\qc \b \i \fs36 English Retelling\b0\i0\par\pard
\qc \b \i \fs26 An original paraphrase of the Grade 6 textbook Fundamentals of the Spiritual and Moral Culture of the Peoples of Russia by Natalya Fyodorovna Vinogradova and Tigran Emilyevich Marinosyan. Moscow: Prosveshcheniye, 2021\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs22 This is an independent condensed retelling, not a quotation or a substitute for the original. Established English forms are used for personal names, place names, institutions, religions, artworks, and historical events. The original work remains copyrighted by the authors and JSC Prosveshcheniye Publishing House.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 About the book\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The source is a sixth-grade Russian school textbook for the subject Fundamentals of the Spiritual and Moral Culture of the Peoples of Russia (commonly abbreviated ODNKNR in Russian, and in academic English sometimes FSMCPR). The authors are Natalya Fyodorovna Vinogradova and Tigran Emilyevich Marinosyan. The 2021 Moscow edition was issued by Prosveshcheniye and authorized by the Ministry of Education of the Russian Federation. It continues the Grade 5 line \u8212? shared human values, the family, tradition, faith, relations among people, and attitudes to nature and the homeland \u8212? and adds the role of schooling in culture, the contribution of Russia\u8217?s traditional religions to morals and the arts, and the artistic culture of the country\u8217?s peoples.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 A letter to pupils maps the year: the family as part of society; moral qualities of a person; traditional religions; and artistic culture \u8212? folklore, literature, the visual arts, architecture, painting, and the decorative and applied arts. The authors invite discussion, research, and project work. A key of icons marks recall tasks, debate, speaking, pair and group work, conclusions, independent work, harder tasks, homework, picture study, historical notes, the four religions, the glossary, and projects.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 Part 1. What the culture of a society is\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 The culture of society\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The lesson asks what \u8220?culture\u8221? means and compares everyday intuition with dictionary entries. Vladimir Dahl traces the word to French senses of tilling, care, and cultivation \u8212? both working the soil and forming mind and morals. Dmitry Ushakov lists several meanings: the sum of human achievements in technology, education, and social order; the condition of a people\u8217?s life in a given age (the culture of Ancient Egypt); \u8220?being cultured\u8221?; plant cultivation (flax, farm crops); laboratory cultures of bacteria; and improvement in general (physical culture).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Culture is then split into material and spiritual. The first covers practical activity \u8212? architecture, tools, household objects, the reshaping of nature. The second covers science, moral rules, religion, and artistic values. The two sides are inseparable: ideas need a material body (a book, a painting, a temple), and objects always carry ideas.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 No one can date the birth of culture: little physical evidence survives, and much of culture is intangible. Each generation selects and hands on what it finds valuable. A people\u8217?s culture is its own system of values; ranking it above all others is a failure of respect. Sources of cultural growth are likewise material (tools, crafts, production) and spiritual (language and writing, the sciences, folklore, literature, custom, religion, and change within the family).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 The human being and faith\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Faith, here, is not only religious. From childhood people trust in something: a gift, a good exam, friendship, justice, honesty. Such trust binds people and makes them more patient with one another. The book cites Anton Pavlovich Chekhov (faith as a capacity of the spirit), the politician Viktor Alekseyevich Zubkov (one cannot live without belief in oneself, in those close to one, in science and progress), and Alexander Sergeyevich Griboyedov\u8217?s line that the believer finds the world warmer. Proverbs warn against rumours and urge trust in one\u8217?s own eyes.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 How belief in God arose. Human beings have long sought the meaning of life and their place in the universe. Scholars place the first traces of religion some 80,000\u8211?50,000 years ago. Early people, afraid of beasts and the elements, animated the world around them \u8212? the root of pagan polytheism. Later came faith in one God; different readings of that faith grew into different religions.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 A table of Russia\u8217?s traditional religions. Eastern Orthodoxy as a distinct branch of Christianity \u8212? second half of the eleventh century; origins in the Roman Empire; present throughout Russia. Islam \u8212? seventh century, the Middle East; in Russia, Tatarstan, Bashkortostan, and some republics of the Caucasus. Judaism \u8212? first half of the first millennium BCE, Palestine; by 2014, about two hundred communities in 178 cities. Buddhism \u8212? mid-first millennium BCE, India; in Russia, Kalmykia, Buryatia, Tuva, Altai, and Transbaikal (Zabaykalye).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Religion is defined as a system of beliefs, values, and rites concerned with the sacred, usually involving trust in a power greater than the human being and worship of that power. Three supports matter to a believer: a place of shared prayer (church, mosque, synagogue, Buddhist temple), practice (prayer, confession, meditation), and everyday moral bearings. Disbelief in God is called atheism; each person is free to choose. An archaeological aside: a 2017 find at Caesarea in Israel, a mother-of-pearl fragment with a menorah from buildings of King Herod (first century BCE).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs25 The four traditional religions\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 A traditional religion is one long practised by peoples living in a given country. For Russia these are Eastern Orthodoxy, Islam, Judaism, and Buddhism. Levada-Center figures for 2012 (the textbook notes that they shift) put Orthodox Christians at about 74 percent, Muslims at 7 percent, and Buddhists and Jews at about 1 percent each.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Christianity arose some two thousand years ago in Palestine. Its founder is Jesus of Nazareth (a town in northern Israel); the teaching stresses mercy, love, and forgiveness. Scholars think the word pravoslavie (Orthodoxy) first appears in Rus' in the eleventh century, in Metropolitan Hilarion of Kiev\u8217?s Sermon on Law and Grace, honouring Prince Vladimir. The sacred book is the Bible (Old and New Testaments). God is understood as the Holy Trinity: Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. The moral core is the Ten Commandments. Early Christian objects shown include a fourth-century glass dish from Andalusia with one of Europe\u8217?s earliest images of Christ and the apostles Peter and Paul, copper icons of the Theotokos, a sixteenth-century double-sided icon of the Crucifixion with St Nicholas, and pectoral crosses of the eleventh to eighteenth centuries.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Islam, from Arabic, means \u8220?submission.\u8221? It is strict monotheism. The Prophet Muhammad was born in Mecca (in present-day Saudi Arabia) in 570 or 571. The sacred book is the Quran (literally \u8220?recitation\u8221?). The Five Pillars are: the profession that Allah is one and Muhammad is His prophet; daily prayer (salah / namaz); fasting in Ramadan (sawm / uraza); almsgiving (zakat); and the pilgrimage to Mecca (hajj) when health and means allow.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Judaism is the national religion of the Jewish people, formed in the first millennium BCE in Palestine. Moses led the tribes out of bondage in Egypt and received revelation; a Covenant was made between the people and the Almighty. The name of God is Yahweh (in the European tradition also Jehovah). The faith includes the immortality of the soul and the expectation of the Messiah. Scripture is the Tanakh: the Torah (Pentateuch), Nevi'im (Prophets), and Ketuvim (Writings). The Sabbath, Shabbat, is a family festival. The menorah, the seven-branched lampstand of the Temple in Jerusalem, is a central symbol.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Buddhism was founded by Siddhartha Gautama around 500 BCE. He left royal luxury and became the Buddha \u8212? the Awakened One. The Four Noble Truths: life involves suffering; the cause is clinging to desire; suffering can cease; the way is moral discipline in this world. Personal responsibility matters more than appeal to a deity. Mantras are inscribed on prayer wheels; turning a wheel is counted as prayer.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 Part 2. Moral values of Russian culture\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 The citizen and the state\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 People live among people, so shared rules are needed. The Constitution of the Russian Federation was adopted by nationwide vote on 12 December 1993. The Preamble speaks in the name of a multinational people bound by a common destiny, rights and freedoms, civic peace, the unity of the state, memory of the ancestors, and responsibility to future generations.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Articles discussed in class. Article 2: the human being, rights and freedoms are the highest value; the state must recognise and protect them. Article 6: equal rights and duties of citizens; citizenship cannot be stripped away. Article 7: Russia is a social state (labour and health protection, a minimum wage, support for families, persons with disabilities, and the elderly). Article 23: privacy and the secrecy of correspondence. Article 26: the right to use one\u8217?s native language. Article 28: freedom of conscience and religion, including the right to profess none. Article 37: free labour, a ban on forced labour, the right to rest. Article 43: the right to education; basic general education is compulsory. Duties include Article 58 \u8212? to protect nature \u8212? and Article 59 \u8212? defence of the Fatherland, with alternative civilian service if military service conflicts with convictions or faith. Project topics include nature reserves and the regional Red Data Book.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Who is called a patriot\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Patriotism is treated as a set of habits, not a slogan: pride in history and in those who defended independence; readiness to defend the country regardless of ethnicity, faith, age, or place of residence; knowledge of the language and of the cultures of Russia\u8217?s peoples; respect for science, literature, and the arts. In daily life it is honest work, interest in culture and sport, help in emergencies, service in the Armed Forces, and participation in community clean-ups and festivals. Pictures range from harvest work to a museum, rescuers, and the Eternal Flame.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Heroic pages of history\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Defence of the homeland is both a legal duty and a moral one. Proverbs liken the land to a mother and insist that a united people cannot be defeated.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs25 The Patriotic War of 1812\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Mikhail Illarionovich Kutuzov was a Field Marshal and commander-in-chief of the Russian army in 1812, formerly military governor of Saint Petersburg and of Kiev; from 1812 he held the title His Serene Highness Prince Golenishchev-Kutuzov-Smolensky. He graduated from the Artillery and Engineering School for the Nobility and spoke several European languages; Madame de Sta\u235?l remarked that he spoke French better than the Corsican Bonaparte. He took part in the storming of Izmail in the Russo-Turkish War of 1787\u8211?1791; Alexander Vasilyevich Suvorov said Kutuzov marched on his left wing but was his right hand. Kutuzov himself said he would not promise victory, but would try to outwit the enemy. After Smolensk he became commander-in-chief and offered general battle at Borodino. He received the Order of St George, First Class, and became the first full cavalier of that order. The textbook portrait is by Roman Maximovich Volkov.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The Battle of Borodino was fought on 26 August 1812 near the village of Borodino, when Napoleon Bonaparte\u8217?s army was about 125 kilometres from Moscow. Emperor Alexander I did not wish to abandon the capital without a fight. Kutuzov chose active defence; the works included the Bagration fl\u232?ches, the Shevardino Redoubt, and the Maslovo fl\u232?ches. The slaughter denied the French a quick victory; the withdrawal toward Moscow is presented not as a rout but as the start of a turning of the tide. Sergei Vasilyevich Gerasimov\u8217?s painting Kutuzov at Borodino is paired with Mikhail Yuryevich Lermontov\u8217?s poem \u8220?Borodino.\u8221?\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 General Nikolay Nikolayevich Raevsky, covering the junction of armies near Smolensk, led the Smolensk Regiment at Saltanovka; family tradition places his sons Alexander (about sixteen) and Nikolay (under fourteen) beside him. The episode is shown in Nikolay Semyonovich Samokish\u8217?s The Feat of Raevsky\u8217?s Soldiers at Saltanovka.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Denis Vasilyevich Davydov was a hussar, poet, and partisan. Suvorov noticed him at the age of nine. In 1807 he was aide-de-camp to Pyotr Ivanovich Bagration and received a gold sabre \u8220?For Bravery.\u8221? On the eve of Borodino he proposed a partisan detachment (about fifty hussars and eighty Cossacks). With Alexander Samoylovich Figner and Alexander Nikitich Seslavin he struck French supply trains; Napoleon is said to have called him the \u8220?black devil.\u8221? Near Lyakhovo, with Orlov-Denisov, they captured a large French force. Davydov wrote An Essay on the Theory of Partisan Action. His portrait is by Orest Adamovich Kiprensky.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Vasilisa Kozhina was the wife of the village elder of the Gorshkovo hamlet in Sychevsky Uyezd of Smolensk Governorate. One version says that, avenging her murdered husband, she raised a band of women and adolescents against French foragers. She is pictured on horseback with a scythe on a shortened shaft. The textbook episode is credited to S. Poshelyuzhny; the painting is Illarion Mikhailovich Pryanishnikov\u8217?s In 1812.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 After Moscow was abandoned, Ivan Semyonovich Dorokhov led a partisan force of hussars, dragoons, and Cossacks, crossed the Protva in secret, and took Vereya. Alexander I presented him with a gold sword. His portrait is by George Dawe. The war of 1812 is called Patriotic because the whole people rose against Napoleon\u8217?s invasion: the regular army, partisans, and civilians.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs25 The Great Patriotic War, 1941\u8211?1945\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The chapter opens with the song \u8220?The Sacred War\u8221? (music by Alexander Vasilyevich Alexandrov, words by Vasily Ivanovich Lebedev-Kumach) and Alexander Alexandrovich Deineka\u8217?s Defence of Sevastopol. Suggested topics include the defence of Moscow, Stalingrad, the Kursk salient, the capture of Berlin, and the Victory Parade.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The home front. More than sixteen million people received the medal \u8220?For Valiant Labour in the Great Patriotic War.\u8221? Women made up more than half of factory workers and about three quarters of those in agriculture. By the end of 1941 hundreds of thousands of senior pupils were working in plants, fields, and hospitals; by 1944 adolescents supplied a large share of the labour force. Public donations paid for aircraft and tanks. The Russian Orthodox Church, at the call of Metropolitan Sergius, raised funds for a tank column named after Dmitry Donskoy. Aircraft designers named are Alexander Sergeyevich Yakovlev, Semyon Alekseyevich Lavochkin, Sergei Vladimirovich Ilyushin, and Andrei Nikolayevich Tupolev; the Red Army\u8217?s chief surgeon was Nikolay Nilovich Burdenko. Among posters is Irakli Toidze\u8217?s The Motherland Calls!\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Awards include the Gold Star medal, the Order of Lenin, the medal \u8220?For Courage\u8221? (more than four million recipients), \u8220?For Combat Merit,\u8221? the Order of the Red Star, the Order of Glory, and the Order of the Patriotic War, First Class. About 11,000 people became Heroes of the Soviet Union; 104 twice; three times \u8212? Georgy Konstantinovich Zhukov, Ivan Nikitovich Kozhedub, and Alexander Ivanovich Pokryshkin. Hero cities named include Moscow, Leningrad (Saint Petersburg), Stalingrad (Volgograd), Novorossiysk, Tula, Smolensk, and Murmansk; the Brest Fortress holds the title Hero Fortress.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Ivan Nikitovich Kozhedub (1920\u8211?1991), a fighter ace at the front from 1943, received three Gold Stars (1944\u8211?1945); more than 300 sorties, over 120 dogfights, 64 aircraft shot down. Lyudmila Mikhailovna Pavlichenko (1916\u8211?1974), a sniper and Hero of the Soviet Union (1943), is credited with 309 enemies and is regarded as the most successful woman sniper in history. Fyodor Kuzmich Popov (1921\u8211?1943) of Yakutia, on 1 October 1943 during the crossing of the Dnieper near Glushets, seized an enemy machine gun, covered his regiment\u8217?s crossing, and killed about seventy soldiers; he died on 13 October; fellow Yakuts funded a tank in his name.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Moral values of the person\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 A human being is both a biological and a social creature. As an individual of the species, a person perceives, remembers, thinks, and grows. As a personality, a person is formed only in contact with others \u8212? in play, work, and study. The social environment, above all parents, is indispensable. A personality plays many roles (child, pupil, friend, pedestrian); individuality is the unique pattern of character.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Moral bearings are illustrated by quotations: Ludwig van Beethoven ranked kindness above other marks of superiority; Vasily Alexandrovich Sukhomlinsky tied love of country to care for another person\u8217?s grief; Nikolay Gavrilovich Chernyshevsky made justice the norm of action; Aristotle held friendship necessary to life; Vladimir Monomakh, in his Instruction, condemned laziness; James Fenimore Cooper likened an idle person to a clock without hands; Saint Nikolaj Velimirovi\u263? (Nikolai of Serbia) warned that stored-up malice poisons the self. Ivan Andreyevich Krylov\u8217?s fables (The Village Assembly, The Cock and the Pearl, The Dog and the Horse) mock injustice, ignorance, and boastfulness.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Conscience is described as an inner voice that still speaks when no one is watching. Will is a trait that can be trained on small aims. Examples of overcoming: the circus artist Valentin Ivanovich Dikul, after a severe spinal injury, devised a rehabilitation method; the dancer Yevgeny Smirnov returned to the stage after losing a leg; the swimmer Olesya Vladykina, after losing an arm, joined the Russian Paralympic team.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 Part 3. The family as creator and guardian of values\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 The family as a cell of society\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Society is made of families; the health of families is a measure of the country. Family functions are emotional (love and understanding), educational, leisure, household-economic, and reproductive (the birth of children).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Article 38 of the Constitution: motherhood, childhood, and the family are under the protection of the state; care for children is an equal right and duty of both parents; adult able-bodied children must support parents who cannot work. The Family Code underpins benefits, free secondary schooling and health care, support for large families, and the Children of Russia programme (including orphans and children with disabilities).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Families differ by composition (two- and three-generation, single-parent) and by number of children. From 1988, on the initiative of the Soviet Children\u8217?s Fund, family-type children\u8217?s homes developed. Tatyana and Mikhail Sorokin of Rostov Oblast raised more than fifty foster children, including sick children and those orphaned by disasters; they received the Order \u8220?For Merit to the Fatherland,\u8221? Second Class, the Order of Honour, and the Golden Heart prize. Russia has more than 180,000 foster parents. Nikolay Petrovich Petrov\u8217?s painting The Bride Show depicts a traditional matchmaking scene in a peasant house.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 A parable of the family hearth: Happiness is about to leave; the master of the house asks only that the hearth not go out \u8212? and Happiness stays, because it lives where the family fire burns.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Family values\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Tatar, Russian, Chechen, Abkhaz, Jewish, and Mordvin proverbs converge: a family stands on harmony, conscience, and work. Diligence, in folk pedagogy, is the great educator. In a peasant household everyone had a task: children herded livestock, carried dinner to the fields, wove bast shoes, spun, and rocked the cradle. Among northern peoples, boys hunted and fished with their fathers; girls ornamented fur clothing and the yaranga. In the Volga region adolescents joined haymaking and the care of horses. Dmitry Ivanovich Mendeleev distinguished labour that can be imposed from work that is conscious, free, and useful to others. Thrift is not greed but respect for other people\u8217?s effort; bread was kept under the icon, crumbs were not thrown away, stale loaves became pies, rusks, and kvass. Paintings: Vladimir Fyodorovich Stozharov, Still Life with Bread; Fyodor Grigoryevich Solntsev, A Peasant Family before Dinner; Aleksey Danilovich Kivshenko, Harvesting.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Kindness and mercy appear in parables (an old woman who spent her life gathering glass on a beach so children would not cut their feet; a brother who asked what Happiness itself needed and pulled it from a pit). Nineteenth-century patronage is summed up as \u8220?wealth obliges.\u8221? Savva Timofeyevich Morozov (1862\u8211?1905) rescued the Moscow Art Theatre, paying for rebuilding and stage machinery; Konstantin Sergeyevich Stanislavsky compared his role to Pavel Mikhailovich Tretyakov\u8217?s for painting. Nikolay Mikhailovich Karamzin wrote of the Slavs\u8217? rare hospitality. Guest customs: among Slavs, bread and salt; among Tatars, chak-chak, sherbet, homemade noodles; in the Caucasus a place is left at table for an unexpected guest; in the North a traveller is given shelter and reindeer meat. The sixteenth-century Domostroy requires work of every member of the household, crafts for sons and needlework for daughters, and forbids lying, envy, and injury.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 The family in the traditional religions\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Eastern Orthodoxy. Marriage is a lifelong bond sealed by the sacrament of crowning (vows, rings, crowns, readings from the Bible). Love is understood as self-giving, after the pattern of Christ. Children are a gift of God; from early years come church, prayer, and Sunday school. Baptism is the first sacrament, entry into the Church; Jesus himself was baptised by John the Baptist in the Jordan. The commandment to honour father and mother includes care for them in old age.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Islam. The family, in this teaching, was established by Allah; the model is the married life of the Prophet Muhammad. A large multi-generational household is typical. According to the Quran, the man is protector and provider; the woman keeps the home and raises the children. Care for the elderly is an honour; nursing homes are rare in Muslim communities. The mother teaches the distinction of halal and haram; the father is responsible for attendance at the mosque; religious schooling continues in the madrasa.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Judaism. Family and home are great gifts; without a family, tradition says, there is no true joy. Father and mother together meet material, educational, and emotional needs. The Torah requires parents to raise upright people; children take part in Shabbat. Honouring parents is a form of honouring God. The synagogue supports families with Hebrew schools and clubs.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Buddhism. Marriage is a contract between people, not a sacred rite; if love has died, divorce is not forbidden, so as not to multiply suffering. At a birth a monk is invited, a horoscope is drawn, a name is given; the infant is brought before a statue of the Buddha. The family is the first school of responsibility for one\u8217?s acts; households practise meditation together and visit monasteries. Elders are valued for wisdom; care for the sick eases suffering.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 Part 4. The role of education in the growth of culture\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Society and education\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The stages shown are kindergarten, school, vocational college, and university. Education raises the productivity of labour, trains specialists, forms a scientific milieu, and lifts the general level of culture. The lesson\u8217?s conclusion: the more educated a society, the more developed it is \u8212? in the economy, in science, and in the arts.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Pages from the history of education\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 In Rus', book learning is tied to the adoption of Christianity. The first schools were at churches and monasteries; before that, teaching was in the family. Tradition holds that in 988 Grand Prince Vladimir Sviatoslavich (Vladimir the Fair Sun) ordered children taken into a school. Schools for girls appear in the eleventh century. Boris Mikhailovich Kustodiev\u8217?s painting A Zemstvo School in Muscovite Rus' illustrates the theme.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Cyril and Methodius, natives of the Byzantine city of Thessalonica (Solun; today Thessaloniki), created a Slavic script in the ninth century, using the Greek alphabet and adding letters for Slavic sounds. They were accused of blasphemy for preaching in Slavic. According to their Life, the idea of the alphabet arose at the Polychron Monastery on Mount Lesser Olympus, where Methodius was abbot. A monument to the brothers stands in Moscow.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Peter I treated education as a condition of military and economic reform. In 1701 the School of Mathematics and Navigation opened (navigation, astronomy, geometry, German). From 1715, cipher (tsifirnye) schools took children aged 10\u8211?15 of every estate except the peasantry; by 1726 there were forty-six. The Slavic Greek Latin Academy in Moscow (1687) marked the start of higher education in Russia; Mikhail Vasilyevich Lomonosov was among its alumni. In 1724 the Academy of Sciences was founded in Saint Petersburg with a gymnasium and a university. In 1731, under Anna Ioannovna, the Land Gentry Cadet Corps opened. In 1755, on Lomonosov\u8217?s design and by decree of Elizabeth Petrovna, Moscow University was founded; teaching was in Russian. Professor Nikolay Nikitich Popovsky held that there is no thought that cannot be expressed in Russian.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Catherine II, following Ivan Ivanovich Betskoy\u8217?s project (the 1764 General Statute on the Education of Both Sexes of Youth), opened a school at the Academy of Arts, foundling hospitals in Moscow and Saint Petersburg, and the Smolny Institute \u8212? the Imperial Educational Society for Noble Maidens, the start of systematic women\u8217?s education in Russia. Smolny pupils studied languages, history, music, dance, and manners. Portraits of Yekaterina Ivanovna Nelidova and Glafira Ivanovna Alymova are by Dmitry Grigoryevich Levitsky. Popular schools (main, middle, and minor) and the classroom-lesson system spread; by the end of the eighteenth century there were hundreds of schools; the Mining School was founded (today the Saint Petersburg Mining University). The textbook also quotes Catherine\u8217?s harsh remark to Moscow\u8217?s governor-general Pyotr Semyonovich Saltykov against educating \u8220?the common people.\u8221?\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The nineteenth century. Literacy at the start of the century was very low. In 1802, under Alexander I, the Ministry of National Enlightenment was created. The ladder ran: parish schools \u8212? district schools \u8212? gymnasiums \u8212? universities (serfs and girls were barred from university). New universities opened in Kharkov, Kazan, Helsinki, Odessa, Warsaw, and Tomsk; by the end of the century there were ten. Classical gymnasiums led to university, real schools to technical institutes. The Forestry Institute, the St Petersburg Institute of Technology, and the Moscow Technical School appeared. Ilya Yefimovich Repin\u8217?s Preparing for the Examination belongs to this chapter.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The Tsarskoye Selo Lyceum opened in 1811 for sons of the nobility. Its pupils included Alexander Sergeyevich Pushkin, Anton Antonovich Delvig, Wilhelm Karlovich K\u252?chelbecker, and Mikhail Lukyanovich Yakovlev. Derzhavin, Zhukovsky, Batyushkov, and Karamzin were prized. In thirty-three years the Lyceum graduated 286 students. On 8 January 1815 the young Pushkin recited \u8220?Recollections at Tsarskoye Selo\u8221? before Gavrila Romanovich Derzhavin \u8212? the day the textbook links with the birth of the \u8220?sun of Russian poetry.\u8221? Repin\u8217?s Pushkin at the Lyceum Examination shows the scene.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Ivan Dmitrievich Sytin (1851\u8211?1934) published cheap books for ordinary readers: Leo Nikolayevich Tolstoy, Nikolay Semyonovich Leskov, Vladimir Galaktionovich Korolenko, self-education series, and textbooks by Konstantin Dmitrievich Ushinsky. Suggested reports include Dmitry Ivanovich Vinogradov (Russian porcelain), Ivan Ivanovich Polzunov (the steam engine), Semyon Ivanovich Chelyuskin (Arctic exploration), and Alexander Stepanovich Popov (radio).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Religion and education\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 After the baptism of Rus', the script of Cyril and Methodius and monastic schools became the base of literacy. From the eleventh century, girls in noble families were taught as well; at St Andrew\u8217?s Convent in Kiev, whose abbess was Princess Anna, sister of Vladimir Monomakh, one of Europe\u8217?s first schools for girls existed. Sophia Palaiologina, niece of a Byzantine emperor and wife of Ivan III, brought to Moscow some eight hundred volumes from the library of Constantinople; they were hidden in the undercroft of the Church of the Nativity of the Theotokos in the Kremlin. That collection is thought to be the seed of the legendary library of Ivan IV the Terrible, whose whereabouts are still sought. Vladimir Monomakh wrote of the soul\u8217?s profit from book learning.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Islam. The Quran was learned by heart at mosques; then schools added Arabic, logic, mathematics, astronomy, and medicine. In the tenth to thirteenth centuries, madrasas and universities in Mecca, Medina, Baghdad, and Cairo set a pattern, including for Europe. In Russia today the Russian Islamic Institute operates in Kazan; madrasas exist in Saint Petersburg, Moscow, Kazan, Ufa, Samara, and Nizhny Novgorod.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Buddhism. Monasteries grew from places of contemplation into schools. The steps are perception, reflection, memorising, the teacher\u8217?s commentary, and debate. The teacher is a guru; in the Tibetan tradition, a lama. The \u8220?greater\u8221? sciences are philosophy, linguistics, logic, technology, and medicine; the \u8220?lesser\u8221? are poetics, music, dance, and astrology. A datsan is a teaching faculty attached to a monastery (Buryatia, Kalmykia).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Judaism. The first school is the family. Sources include the Bible, the Pentateuch of Moses, the Book of Proverbs, and the Talmud (patience, regard for a child\u8217?s abilities). Institutions: the synagogue, the Talmud Torah (from the fourteenth\u8211?fifteenth centuries), the yeshiva, and rabbinical seminaries. Russia has a Jewish University in Saint Petersburg; day and Sunday schools operate in Moscow, Saint Petersburg, Nizhny Novgorod, Saratov, Irkutsk, and Birobidzhan.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 Part 5. Artistic culture of the peoples of Russia\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Fields of artistic culture\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The opening picture is Jean-Baptiste-Sim\u233?on Chardin\u8217?s The Attributes of the Arts. Only human beings create: they can invent what does not yet exist. The source is the power to be astonished. The arts are grouped as spatial (architecture, graphic art, sculpture, painting, decorative and applied art), temporal (literature, music, poetry), and synthetic (theatre, cinema, choreography, circus, television). Artistic culture forms taste and ideas of the good and the beautiful.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Myth (from Greek \u8220?tale\u8221?) explains the world\u8217?s order and the human place in it. The Slavic pantheon in the book: Svarog, sky god who gave the plough and smithing; Perun, thunderer and patron of the prince\u8217?s retinue (the Kiev idol with a silver head); Bereginya, who with Rod brings forth life \u8212? her festival the rusalia, her tree the birch; Lada, love and household concord (folk etymology links her name with blini-like oladyi); Veles (Volos), cattle, poetry, and the cycle of the seasons; Svyatobor, forest and hunting; Svyatogor, the bylina giant. Paintings: Ivan Yakovlevich Bilibin, Ilya Muromets and Svyatogor; Nicholas Konstantinovich Roerich, Svyatogor.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Decorative and applied art\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The photo sequence includes a Pavlovsky Posad shawl, the matryoshka, birch-bark tuessa, northern bone carving, Kubachi silver, Gzhel, a Zhostovo tray, the Dymkovo toy, and the \u8220?bird of happiness.\u8221? Slavic embroidery is not only ornament but a charm: collar, hem, sleeves, belt, and a button with a \u8220?sun sign\u8221? were meant to protect. Decorative and applied art joins use and beauty.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 A riddle about a people points to the Yakuts (Sakha). Fish and stroganina; reindeer, seal, and Arctic-fox meat; berry drinks of lingonberry, cloudberry, and bilberry. Fur clothing with geometric ornament; bone-carved figures. The chief festival is Ysyakh, the meeting of summer: the ritual round dance (osuokhay), the khomus (jaw harp), jumping, wrestling, archery, and kumis. The textbook\u8217?s reminder: a citizen should know the cultures of the peoples of the country.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Outstanding figures\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Isaac Ilyich Levitan (1860\u8211?1900) found beauty in quiet, unshowy landscape (A June Day). Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky (1840\u8211?1893) heard music in a thunderstorm, a winter road, and a Moscow garden; the cycle The Seasons. The gallery then names the ballerina Anna Pavlovna (Matveyevna) Pavlova; Alexander Sergeyevich Pushkin and the monument by Alexander Mikhailovich Opekushin on Strastnaya (now Pushkin) Square in Moscow; Feodor Ivanovich Chaliapin as Boris Godunov; the Chechen dancer Makhmud Alisultanovich Esambayev; the architect Matvey Fyodorovich Kazakov and the Petrovsky Palace; the sculptor Vera Ignatyevna Mukhina, author of Worker and Kolkhoz Woman; the Erzya sculptor Stepan Dmitrievich Erzia; the founders of the Moscow Art Theatre, Konstantin Sergeyevich Stanislavsky and Vladimir Ivanovich Nemirovich-Danchenko; and the Avar poet Rasul Gamzatovich Gamzatov.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Artistic culture born of religion\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The Orthodox church. After the baptism of Rus', churches rose in Kiev, Pskov, Novgorod, Vladimir, and Moscow, at first on Byzantine models. The plan may suggest a ship of salvation, a cross, or the circle of eternity. The onion dome is likened to a candle flame. Three parts lie on a west\u8211?east axis: narthex, nave (\u8220?ship\u8221?), and sanctuary behind the iconostasis with the Royal Doors. An example is the Cathedral of Christ the Saviour in Moscow.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The mosque is a \u8220?place of worship\u8221?: a court with water for ablution, minarets from which the muezzin calls to prayer. There are no images of people or animals; walls carry geometric and vegetal ornament and calligraphy. An example is the Heart of Chechnya Mosque in Grozny (the Akhmad Kadyrov Mosque).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Buddhist buildings: the stupa (relics of awakening), the pagoda, the datsan. The temple is related to the five elements. At the entrance palms are joined and bows are made in multiples of three. Prayer wheels, roof bells, and a statue of the Buddha belong to the setting.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The synagogue is a house of prayer and a centre of communal life (some twenty-five centuries). The fa\u231?ade faces Jerusalem; men and women have separate areas; there is a raised place for reading the Torah, an ark for the scrolls, a menorah, and tablets of the Ten Commandments. An example is the Grand Choral Synagogue in Saint Petersburg.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 The arts of the traditional religions\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The icon. Pavel Alexandrovich Florensky wrote that the icon-painter must be humble, fast, and pray: this is church service, not \u8220?just painting.\u8221? The icon exists for prayer and preaching. The Theotokos is especially venerated; the Kazan Icon is linked with the liberation of Moscow in 1612, the expulsion of Napoleon, and the defence of Moscow and Leningrad in 1941. The iconostasis is spoken of as \u8220?windows\u8221? and \u8220?bridges\u8221? into heaven. Bell ringing is called a \u8220?singing icon\u8221?: blagovest, perebor, perezvon, trezvon. In their youth Chaliapin, Suvorov, Chekhov, and Lomonosov sang in church choirs. The belfry of St Sophia in Veliky Novgorod is one of the chapter\u8217?s images.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Islamic art. \u8220?Eyes do not attain Him\u8221? \u8212? therefore Allah is not depicted; animals too are avoided, lest a person rival the Creator. The chief language is calligraphy and the arabesque: an endless vegetal-geometric rhythm on portals, cornices, carpets, and books.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Buddhism. Early symbols: the Buddha\u8217?s footprint, the Bodhi tree, the lotus, the wheel of teaching (dharmachakra). A mandala is a map of the universe for meditation. A sand mandala is deliberately swept away when finished: a reminder of impermanence; the sand is thrown into running water.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Judaism. The Second Commandment forbids idols, so there are no icons as objects of worship. Art concentrates on the synagogue\u8217?s furnishings and sacred objects: the Torah crown, the pointer, the menorah. A mosaic example is the synagogue at Sepphoris (Tzippori) in Israel, with a zodiac circle. The Star of David, by tradition once on the shields of King David\u8217?s warriors, appears on the flag of Israel. Ivan Konstantinovich Aivazovsky turned to biblical subjects: The Creation of the World, The Flood, Noah Descending from Mount Ararat, Walking on Water.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The section\u8217?s close: Russia\u8217?s artistic culture draws on two sources \u8212? the traditions of its peoples and the traditional religions, which carry both their own and shared human values.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 Part 6. How to preserve the culture of society\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Who is called an educated person\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The textbook rejects two shortcuts: \u8220?an educated person is someone who knows everything\u8221? and \u8220?an educated person is someone with a degree.\u8221? Closer to the truth is a third view: the educated person keeps learning, commands speech and languages, and can think and argue. The gallery of \u8220?educated geniuses\u8221? includes Mikhail Vasilyevich Lomonosov, the polymath; Dmitry Ivanovich Mendeleev, compared with Leonardo da Vinci; Nikolay Ivanovich Lobachevsky, six times rector of Kazan University; Sofia Vasilyevna Kovalevskaya, the first woman professor of mathematics and a corresponding member of the St Petersburg Academy of Sciences; Zhores Ivanovich Alferov, Nobel laureate in physics for work on semiconductors; Lev Davidovich Landau, a child prodigy and Nobel laureate; Vladimir Mikhailovich Bekhterev, of whom the German anatomist Kopsch said that only God and Bekhterev knew the structure of the brain perfectly; and Lev Semyonovich Vygotsky, the \u8220?Mozart of psychology,\u8221? with a theory of learning and the development of speech.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Cultural heritage\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Heritage is that part of culture which has stood the test of time and is handed on with care. It may be tangible (palaces, books, paintings, temples) or intangible (languages, rites, crafts, the laws of an art). The most valued sites enter the UNESCO World Heritage List. Andrei Rublev\u8217?s icon-painting is given as an example of Russian culture with worldwide resonance.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The Moscow Kremlin is an ensemble of the history of the Russian state; the Tsar Cannon and Tsar Bell, walls and towers; a UNESCO site. Lake Baikal in Eastern Siberia is the world\u8217?s deepest lake, holding about 19 percent of the planet\u8217?s fresh water. Kizhi Pogost in Karelia, on an island in Lake Onega: two wooden churches and a bell tower; the 22-domed Church of the Transfiguration, by legend built without nails; the Kizhi Museum-Reserve. Veliky Novgorod: Yaroslav\u8217?s Court and the Marketplace (Torg), the Novgorod Kremlin (Detinets), the Vladychnaya Chamber (Archbishop\u8217?s Palace), and the Church of the Saviour on Nereditsa (1198, Novgorod school, frescoes; Nereditsa is a river).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Why protect. Monuments are the contribution of Russia\u8217?s peoples to world culture \u8212? not only costly objects, but a spiritual way of seeing. Threats include the black market, looting of excavations, demolition of historic buildings, and theft of paintings. The 2002 federal law On Objects of Cultural Heritage (Monuments of History and Culture) of the Peoples of the Russian Federation obliges citizens to care for heritage. Protection includes research, restoration, and conservation. Helpers include the All-Russian Society for the Protection of Monuments of History and Culture, Archnadzor in Moscow, and Zhivoy Gorod (\u8220?Living City\u8221?) in Saint Petersburg. Rules on a heritage site: no graffiti, no breaking off souvenirs, no campfires, take litter away, do no harm to living nature.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 Glossary (paraphrased senses)\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Ayah \u8212? a verse of the Quran.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Guberniya (governorate) \u8212? a large administrative unit of Russia from the time of Peter I.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Disputation \u8212? a learned debate in search of truth; a required method in Buddhist schooling.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Hegumen (abbot) \u8212? the superior of an Orthodox monastery.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Idol \u8212? a carved image of a pagan deity.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Chamber (as in chamber music) \u8212? meant for a small room and a small audience.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Canon \u8212? a standing rule, a traditional model.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Cornice \u8212? a horizontal projection of a wall beneath the roof.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Code \u8212? a body of law in one field.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Cultivation (of soil) \u8212? loosening the earth and cutting weeds.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Linguistics \u8212? the science of languages.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Madrasa \u8212? a Muslim secondary and higher school.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Patron (Maecenas) \u8212? a person who freely gives private means to science and art.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Muezzin \u8212? a mosque official who calls Muslims to prayer.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Navigation \u8212? seafaring.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Pedagogy \u8212? the science of upbringing and teaching.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Full cavalier \u8212? a holder of every class of an order.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Portal \u8212? a formally designed main entrance.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Thrift (rachitelnost) \u8212? an older word for care, economy, and attentiveness.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Surah \u8212? a chapter of the Quran.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Texture \u8212? the inner structure of a material (cloth, wood, stone).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Theology \u8212? the body of religious teaching; divinity.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Uyezd \u8212? a subdivision of a governorate.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Elite \u8212? the upper layer of a society (political, economic, or cultural).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Yaranga \u8212? a tent-like dwelling of some peoples of the Far North and Siberia.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Cell (as in \u8220?cell of society\u8221?) \u8212? a small unit among others of the same kind (as the family in society).\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs32 Closing note\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 The Grade 6 course traces a chain: culture as a society\u8217?s memory and making \u8212? faith and Russia\u8217?s traditional religions \u8212? citizenship and patriotism, tested in 1812 and in 1941\u8211?1945 \u8212? personality, conscience, and will \u8212? the family as keeper of values \u8212? school and the history of enlightenment \u8212? the arts of peoples and religions \u8212? responsibility for heritage. The through-line of this retelling: to know one\u8217?s own culture and to respect another\u8217?s is not an ornament of erudition, but a condition of life in a multinational society.\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs28 Note on names in the English version\b0\i0\par\pard
\qj \b \i \fs24 Where an established English form exists, it is used rather than a fresh transliteration. Examples: Moscow, Saint Petersburg, Veliky Novgorod, Tsarskoye Selo, Lake Baikal, Kizhi Pogost, Church of the Saviour on Nereditsa, Cathedral of Christ the Saviour, Heart of Chechnya Mosque; Pushkin, Tchaikovsky, Chaliapin, Tolstoy, Dostoevsky is not in this volume, but parallel cases include Roerich (not Rerikh), Erzia (English Wikipedia form of \u1069?\u1088?\u1100?\u1079?\u1103?), Esambayev, Gamzatov, Aivazovsky, Alferov, Vygotsky; Quran, Torah, Tanakh, Nevi'im, Ketuvim, Shabbat, Hajj, Ysyakh, Siddhartha Gautama, Theotokos, UNESCO. Historical offices of the Russian Empire keep conventional English historiography (Kiev Military Governor for 1812; Kharkov University in the nineteenth-century list). The publisher is Prosveshcheniye. The school subject\u8217?s established academic English is Fundamentals of the Spiritual and Moral Culture of the Peoples of Russia.\b0\i0\par\pard
